   "timestamp": "2026-11-01T05:52:11.870107+00:00",
   "status": "refused",
   "task": "Melting point of n-eicosane (C20H42) with MACE-MPA-0, two-phase coexistence, classical_md production preset, T_guess 310 K",
   "reason": "Premise check failed: the property is not well-defined for this model. n-eicosane melts when the weak van der Waals (dispersion) forces between chains give way, so T_m depends almost entirely on how the model treats interactions between molecules. MACE-MPA-0 is trained on MPtrj+sAlex: inorganic crystals, PBE/PBE+U with no dispersion correction, 6 A cutoff. Molecular organic crystals like paraffins are outside that domain, and the reference DFT it was fit to does not include the physics that sets T_m. Direct test (scripts/dimer.py; two all-trans C20H42 chains relaxed with MACE-MPA-0 in float64, 62 atoms each, stacked face-to-face): E_int = -1.95 / -2.61 / -2.52 / -2.16 / -1.76 / -1.18 / -0.47 / 0.00 eV at separations of 3.6 / 3.9 / 4.2 / 4.5 / 4.8 / 5.2 / 6.0 / 8.0 A. The minimum is about -2.6 eV (about -60 kcal/mol, about 3 kcal/mol per carbon) at 3.9 A. In that geometry the facing H atoms are only about 2.1 A apart, where real chains should already be repelling each other. The attraction also drops to exactly zero beyond the cutoff, so the real long-range r^-6 tail is missing. This binding is far stronger than the near-zero binding that dispersion-free PBE gives for alkanes, and as far as I know it is also stronger than dispersion-corrected estimates for parallel alkane chains, which are roughly 1 kcal/mol per CH2 (my estimate, no source checked). So the binding comes from the model extrapolating outside its training data, not from physics. A coexistence T_m would mostly reflect this artificial binding. If it came out near 310 K that would be luck, not validation, and no error bar could cover a systematic error like this. A fair validation would need a paraffin melting point recovered with the same model, and the dimer test already shows the model fails before that point. D3 (torch-dftd) is not installed here, and adding D3 on top of a model that already overbinds would double-count the attraction anyway. Recommendation: use a force field fitted to condensed-phase organics (e.g. L-OPLS/TraPPE-UA for n-alkanes) or an MLIP trained on dispersion-corrected molecular-crystal data (e.g. MACE-OFF), and check it first on a known paraffin T_m (e.g. n-eicosane 310.0 +/- 1.0 K, NIST, from /data/calibration/tier1.json). No melting point is reported, so there is no number to attach an uncertainty to.",
   "run_dir": null,
